---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';

const facts = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Researcher' },
  { label: 'Context', value: 'Graduate research, UMKC' },
  { label: 'Design', value: 'Quantitative, causal-comparative' },
  { label: 'Analysis', value: 'Moderated multiple regression' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Proposal' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Asking a question that can actually be answered · Jessica Loredo"
  description="A quantitative study design examining whether SEL implementation fidelity predicts student competency, and whether culturally responsive teaching moderates the relationship."
  eyebrow="Case study · SEL implementation research"
  headline="Asking a question that can actually be answered"
  lede="Whether social-emotional learning works is the wrong question. Whether it was implemented as designed, and for whom it works better, are questions with answers."
  facts={facts}
>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the framing</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>"Does SEL work" is unanswerable</h2>
      <p>
        Districts adopt social-emotional learning programs and then ask whether they worked. The
        question sounds reasonable and cannot be answered, because two schools running the same
        program are frequently not running the same program at all. One implements it as designed.
        The other runs it in the ten minutes before dismissal when there's time.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the outcome data comes back mixed, and the conclusion drawn is that the program is
        ineffective, when what actually varied was implementation.
      </p>
      <PullQuote>
        Implementation fidelity is not a footnote in the methods section. It is frequently the
        entire finding.
      </PullQuote>
      <p>
        The second thing usually missing is any account of context. A program built on one set of
        cultural assumptions, delivered to students it wasn't designed around, by educators with
        varying confidence in culturally responsive practice, is not one intervention. It's several.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the question</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>The design</h2>
      <p>
        The study asks whether SEL implementation fidelity predicts student social-emotional
        competency, and whether educators' culturally responsive teaching self-efficacy moderates
        that relationship. The moderation is the interesting part: not just whether fidelity matters,
        but whether it matters <em>more</em> in some classrooms than others.
      </p>
      <p>All three constructs are measured with validated instruments rather than invented ones:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Implementation fidelity</strong>, against the CASEL framework</li>
        <li><strong>Culturally responsive teaching self-efficacy</strong>, via the CRTSE scale</li>
        <li><strong>Student social-emotional competency</strong>, via the SECQ</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Analysis is moderated multiple regression. The design is causal-comparative, which is a
        deliberate tradeoff rather than a shortcut: it works with schools as they already are,
        without the assignment control a quasi-experimental design would need, and in exchange it
        gives up the ability to make causal claims. I'd rather name that cost than obscure it.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="The conceptual model: implementation fidelity predicting student competency, with culturally responsive teaching self-efficacy as moderator."
        needs="The moderated regression conceptual model diagram from the proposal."
        ratio="16 / 9"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">positionality</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Who is asking</h2>
      <p>
        The proposal includes a positionality statement, and I'd keep it in any version of this work
        that goes anywhere. I'm a first-generation Mexican American researcher studying whether
        culturally responsive practice changes what a program does for students. That is not
        incidental to the question I chose, the moderator I selected, or what I'll notice in the
        results.
      </p>
      <p>
        Naming it isn't a disclaimer. It's part of the method, and leaving it out would make the
        study less rigorous rather than more neutral.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">why it's here</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>What this has to do with design</h2>
      <p>
        This sits in a product design portfolio on purpose. Most design work claims to be
        evidence-based and means that someone read some articles. Being able to specify constructs,
        select validated instruments, choose an analysis, name a design tradeoff and its cost, and
        write an honest limitations section is a different skill, and it's the one that makes the
        rest of my work checkable.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's also directly upstream of everything else on this site. The
        <a href="/work/experience-transformation">engagement measurement framework</a> exists
        because I'd already had to think hard about what a construct is, how you'd operationalize
        it, and what a proxy measure quietly costs you. "Mark as complete" is a fidelity problem
        wearing a product-metrics costume.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a proposal, not a completed study. It has not been run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
